      "writtenOn": "2025-11-18T14:19:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Ok, I think I got it: those hypercalls are actually avoided by the previous patch aosp/3786912?\n\nYes correct. Since the translation structures are in memory, we wouldn\u0027t be doing MMIO until we get to SRTP and TE\n\n\u003e Still, it is, like, a few dozen hypercalls, not a few thousand? So their actual impact on the boot time may be negligible?\n\nYes, probably about 3 hypercalls per device probed. Thinking more about it, it might be negligeble yes.\n\n\u003e ...Looking at the changes in this patch, I\u0027m just not entirely sure it is 100% correct to move iommu_flush_write_buffer() after enabling the IRQ in dmar_set_interrupt()\n\nFrom reading the VT-d spec(6.8), I think we should be safe. For recent hardwares, write buffer flushing is not required and for older ones, its required only on updating non-present entries in the in-memory structures. Present entry updates require other cache flushing(context/pasid) and write buffer flush will happen with it. In our case, we do the flush before setting root entry and I think dmar faults would not happen until we enable translation.\n\n\u003e or to not care what exactly the comment about \"some Intel X58 chipsets\" means, and so on.\n\nThe comment about X58 is about setting RTP in all iommus in a multi iommu system. Without that QI was not working in X58 boards: https://github.com/torvalds/linux/commit/a4c34ff1c029e90e7d5f8dd8d29b0a93b31c3cb2\n\nAlso looks like they used to set root entry right before translation enable, but moved it back probably for supporting kexec(kdump) https://github.com/torvalds/linux/commit/5f0a7f7614a9d99325ac8d618f1cdf7a3014287c\n\nWhile looking at kdump implementation, what they do is copy over existing translation structures into the new one and then switch the RTP. I think it would be okay to keep the old one until TE and would not make a difference. Since we do not support kdump for now, I was planning to revisit that later. I think there is an oppurtunity to cleanup code a bit for upstream in this regard. BTW, I haven\u0027t yet got to disabling kdump while initializing pkvm(disable iommu explicitly) so that host driver is not confused. That will be a separate temp patch and will go away when we have kdump support with pkvm.\n\n\u003e That said, most probably these changes are 100% safe for our use cases anyway, since iommu_flush_write_buffer() does nothing on the hardware we care about, and so on. So it should be fine if we merge it. I\u0027m just not so sure e.g. about future upstreaming of them. (Actually a scenario I\u0027m more worried about is: these changes are actually accepted upstream, and then they break some setups for someone, and that is our fault.)\n\nYeah upstreaming is definitely a different beast. As mentioned above, I have some plans on tweaking the implementation(hopefully without breaking others). I think I will do it with 6.18 as 6.18 is a bit different and we need a slightly different implementation in 6.18 anyways\n\n\u003e OTOH, iommu_set_root_entry() and iommu_enable_translation() as is don\u0027t return any errors, while pKVM might return errors in those hypercalls (so from this POV it might be good to consolidate IOMMU enablement in a single point in the IOMMU driver initialization code, to let it easy fail initialization if needed)... OTOH, should those errors really happen if the host is using those hypercalls correctly (considering that during the IOMMU driver initialization the host is still trusted)?\n\nYes, this implementation is fully based on the assumption that host is trusted when this initialization happens and it shouldn\u0027t fail unless there is some hardware issues or pkvm issues(memory or such).\n\n\u003e So, from this perspective, it might be attractive to just add calling corresponding hypercalls to iommu_set_root_entry() and iommu_enable_translation(), without reshuffling the usage of iommu_set_root_entry() and iommu_enable_translation() throughout the code (and thus without the need to make assumptions if this reshuffling is fully correct).\n\nYeah I agree, its an assumption at this point that teh reshuffling is okay :-) (based on code and googling and testing various scenarios). It might be wrong and there might be corner cases as well. I am fine with changing this as you suggested. As you mentioned, once you parse the whole series, let me know what you think and we will take it from there.",
